{"seconds": 93.3, "exit": 0, "final": "After Rg2+ Rg4 Rxg4+ Kh6, the rook on g4 is attacked by the h5 pawn. White could play Rg6+ to give check and drive the king further.\n\nBut actually, let me compare:\n- **Rg2+ Kh4 Kxf4** \u2014 White wins the rook, up +5 material. No more moves needed.\n- **Rg2+ Kh6 Kxf4** \u2014 White wins the rook, up +5 material. No more moves needed.\n- **Rg2+ Rg4 Rxg4+** \u2014 White trades rooks. Then the king moves and the White rook is still attacked by the h5 pawn.\n\nThe first two lines are clearly best. So **Rg2+** is the best move, and the main line is **Rg2+ Kh4 Kxf4** (or Kh6 with the same result).\n\nLet me verify Rg2+ is SAN correctly. The rook is on h2 and moves to g2 with check. So it's Rg2+.\n\n**Coaching Note (max 80 words):**\n\n\"After your king moved to g5, White had a forcing sequence you missed. The key was Rg2+, giving check without losing the rook. Your king had to move\u2014if Kh4, Kh6, or other squares, your rook on f4 would become undefended and White captures it with Kxf4, winning the game. The lesson: always check if a piece is still defended after the opponent moves their king. A piece defended one move can become loose the next.\"\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Rg2+\", \"line\": [\"Rg2+\", \"Kh4\", \"Kxf4\"], \"motifs\": [\"deflection\", \"hangingPiece\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "8/5p2/4p3/4P1kp/5r2/4K3/P6R/8 w - - 15 57"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "8/5p2/4p3/4P1kp/5r2/4K3/P6R/8 w - - 15 57"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "8/5p2/4p3/4P1kp/5r2/4K3/P6R/8 w - - 15 57"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/5p2/4p3/4P1kp/5r2/4K3/P6R/8 w - - 15 57", "move": "Rxh5"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/5p2/4p3/4P1kp/5r2/4K3/P6R/8 w - - 15 57", "move": "Rg2"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/5p2/4p3/4P1kR/5r2/4K3/P7/8 b -